  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "SUFFICIENT",
    "confidence": 91,
    "conclusion": "The evidence supports semantic stability under nominally identical repetition: across 16 anchors repeated five times, no semantic-class instability was observed. Observable responses can nevertheless vary in broader state dimensions, so instability claims should be qualified rather than generalized. Prompt paraphrasing and prior-answer context meaningfully increased full-state response distance without producing semantic flips; the changes instead involved claim state, action, evidence, uncertainty, assertion, referent, and abstention/request behavior. Measurement reliability strongly supports the semantic-class result and reasonably supports action changes, but conclusions about claim state, abstention, exact referents, and exact evidence sets should be treated more cautiously because scorer agreement was only moderate to poor or partial. Reject explanations that nominally identical questions inherently cause unstable semantic answers, or that observed variation necessarily reflects semantic reversal. Retain explanations based on sensitivity of nonsemantic response state to wording and conversational history, while allowing that some apparent variation—especially in weakly reliable dimensions—may reflect measurement noise."
  },
